{
  "sticker": "pelican",
  "skeleton": "pelican standing in three-quarter view facing left: root between the webbed feet; body (with the long neck) with belly squash; head at the top of the neck, face carrying the single eye, brow and the big beak with its pouch (swapped for open and full); near wing folded on the back (open in flight), the far wing shown only open in flight (flipped, shaded); legL/legR at the hips.",
  "sheets": [
    {
      "id": "parts",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a pelican character. Draw this exact pelican character as a cut-out paper puppet: its separate body parts laid out on one sheet, the same drawing style, colours, texture, line and proportions as in image 1, every part at the size it has in image 1.\n\nDraw each part on its own, spread out over the sheet with wide empty gaps between them, no part touching or overlapping another:\n1. the body: the fluffy white body with its long curved neck, facing left as in image 1, without head, wings or legs; the top of the neck, normally under the head, rounded.\n2. the head: the round white head with its little tuft of feathers on top, facing left as in image 1, with a completely blank face — no eye, no brow, no beak — keep the rosy cheek; its bottom edge rounded.\n3. the beak: the long orange beak with its big pale orange pouch hanging under it, closed, as in image 1, with a rounded base.\n4. the beak open: the same beak with its top bill lifted wide open and the empty pouch hanging below it, with a rounded base.\n5. the beak full: the same beak closed, its pouch swollen big, round and heavy, full of water, with a rounded base.\n6. the folded wing: the grey-and-white wing with its dark tips, folded as it lies on the body in image 1, drawn whole, with a rounded shoulder.\n7. the open wing: the same wing spread out wide as in flight, its long grey and dark flight feathers fanned, with a rounded shoulder.\n8. one leg: an orange leg with its big webbed foot, drawn whole from the top of the leg, with a rounded top.\n\nEvery part is drawn whole, including what is normally hidden behind another part, and each joint end (where it tucks under the body or the head) is a full, smooth, rounded edge. No text, no labels, no numbers, no outline around the parts beyond the drawing's own line, no shadows. Fully transparent background."
    },
    {
      "id": "face",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "references": [
        "parts"
      ],
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a pelican character; image 2 shows the same pelican cut into puppet parts, with a blank face. Draw the pelican's face pieces as separate stickers on one sheet: the same drawing style, colours and line as in image 1, every piece at the size it has on the pelican in image 1, so it fits the blank face in image 2.\n\nDraw each piece on its own, spread out in tidy rows with wide empty gaps between them, nothing touching:\n1. the pelican's eye as it is in image 1: a dark brown round eye with a white sparkle.\n2. the same eye wide open and surprised: bigger and rounder, with its white sparkle.\n3. the same eye gently closed in sleep: a soft downward-curving line with a few lashes.\n4. the same eye closed in a happy smile: an upward-curving arc.\n5. the same eye sad: the pupil looking down, its upper lid drooping and tilted, a thin shine of a welling tear along the bottom rim.\n6. the same eye crying: gently squeezed shut, a soft downward-curving line (a sad closed eye, not a smile), a few small lashes, and one small light-blue teardrop just below it.\n7. the brow: the short brown curved brow line above the eye in image 1.\n\nNo text, no labels, no numbers, no shadows, no face or head around the pieces. Fully transparent background."
    }
